The docstring says "맨 위로 되돌아가는 두 번째 걷기가 필요 없다" (no second walk required). But the test requires a second walk. Hmm, but the description in test is about when the sheet closes, the list jumps to top, and we need to walk from bottom again to get middle items.

Now let's design: detect that after an action the list reset to the top. When prev key is identical to a previously seen key (i.e., we've seen this screen before and page didn't advance), we should scroll to bottom again and re-walk rather than swiping back repeatedly.

Simplest: track seen screen keys. On each turn, if the current screen key was already visited in this walk (from an earlier page), and there's still progress possible... Actually the situation: page 0 read screen {글8,글9}; after handling 글9, the list reset to top. Next turn page1 reads {글0,글1} which hasn't been seen (page 0 was 글8,글9). So not a repeat. Then swipe back (which in fake does nothing at top) → page 2 reads {글0,글1} again → key repeats prev → stuck. With stuck>=2 break. Then the remaining pending 글5,글4 marked as not found. Result: skipped includes them.

To handle: when "stuck" is detected at the top (can't go further up), go to bottom and start a fresh pass. But when at top we can't know we're at top except by no change after swipe.

Test: "진전이 없으면 다시 걷지 않고 못 찾은 글만 건너뛴다" - 6 titles, pending "없는 글" which doesn't exist. So walk must terminate. With restart logic, we'd loop forever (or until turn limit). We need to cap restarts and rely on not re-tapping already-attempted titles (pending deleted on attempt). The pending set shrinks; each title attempted once. "없는 글" never found, so after a full pass with no new titles seen, stop.

Approach: track visited screen keys (Set). If the current screen key is already in visited set, we've looped around the list — need to go to bottom for another pass but only if something might still be unfound; but to avoid infinite loops, allow at most N passes (e.g., one more pass after reset). Also the "진전 없음" test: screen {글0,글1} repeated → visited → currently we'd... hmm let's think.

Actually simpler heuristic closer to real device behavior: after closing a sheet (backToSales), the list may reset to top. We can detect that in walkUp after an action: re-read screen at the start of next turn. If the new screen key equals a screen key seen earlier (i.e., jump backwards), then reset: scrollToEdge bottom again and continue. In the test, after handling 글9 at page0 (screen 글8,글9 → top reset to 0). Next turn page1 reads 글0,글1 — not previously visited, so we do NOT detect reset. Hmm.

Would it be better to detect after the action within the same turn: after bumpOne returns and backToSales executed, read current screen and compare to what we expect (the same screen as the dump we were processing)? If it differs, re-dump and restart from bottom. But dumps are expensive; still, maybe use the next turn's read which we already do.

Alternative: don't rely on reset detection; change walk direction strategy: instead of walking only upward, restart from the bottom each time an item is handled (since bump moves items to top and sheet close resets to top). But that costs lots of dumps.

Let's think about what a correct algorithm is that passes both tests and is sensible:

Keep list of "screen keys" seen in current pass. If a turn's key has been seen before in this pass, that means swiping "back" didn't move (we're at the top). Then, if there are still pending items, scroll to bottom and start a new pass. Limit passes to e.g. 3 or until no progress in a pass. Also avoid re-attempting handled ones (pending deleted).

For the reset test: pass 1: page0 screen {글8,글9} → handle 글9 (skip). swipe back → top=0 → page1 {글0,글1} → not pending, key new → swipe back (no move) → page2 {글0,글1} key seen → restart: scrollToEdge bottom → page3 {글8,글9} key seen (seen in pass)?? We need per-pass seen keys and reset on new pass. New pass clears seen. page3 {글8,글9} → not pending (글9 deleted) → swipe back → page4 {글0,글1}... hmm wait, at bottom after scrollToEdge, the walk goes up: page3 = bottom screen {글8,글9}, swipe back moves up by 2 → {글6,글7} → {글4,글5} contains 글5 → handle 글5, but backToSales resets to top=0! Then next turn reads {글0,글1}. Then swipe back no move → repeats → restart from bottom: {글8,글9} (seen? new pass) → swipe up to {글6,글7} → {글4,글5} contains 글4 → handle. Then another pass to find nothing → terminate. So passes: 3. We need a pass limit maybe MAX_PASSES = 3 or 4, or stop when a pass makes no progress (no item attempted). Pass structure:

Loop with passes. Each pass: scrollToEdge bottom, then walk up until stuck/no movement. Track whether any item handled in the pass; if none, stop (no progress). That handles the "못 찾은 글" test: pass 1: 6 titles {글0..글5}, pending 없는 글. scrollToEdge bottom → top=maxTop=4 → screen {글4,글5}. swipe back → top=2 → {글2,글3} → swipe back → {글0,글1} → swipe back → top stays 0 → repeated key twice → pass ends with no progress → stop. That takes 2 extra turns with same key, fine.

But how much does the existing test time budget allow? Tests have 120s timeouts; each dump sleep maybe ~? settledDump probably sleeps. Test currently takes 3.5s for that one. Doubling could be fine.

But maybe simpler: rather than restarting from bottom each pass, only detect reset via "key seen in this pass" and if so restart from bottom immediately (without waiting for stuck==2). In the reset test page2 sees repeat key → restart from bottom, new pass. In the no-progress test page after top repeat → restart from bottom? That would loop: pass 2 from bottom reads {글4,글5}, swipes to {글2,글3}, {글0,글1}, repeat → restart... forever until turn limit 60. With progress tracking: a pass with no handled item → stop. So implement: when repeat detected → if this pass handled ≥1 item, restart from bottom; else stop.

